AI Lab become a hive of radical experimentation. Under Minsky\u2019s leadership, researchers built some of the first AI systems capable of understanding natural language, solving geometric analogies, and playing games. Terry Winograd\u2019s SHRDLU (1968\u20131970), a program that could manipulate blocks in a simulated world using natural language commands, demonstrated that machines could engage in context-sensitive reasoning\u2014a milestone that inspired generations of NLP research. Meanwhile, Rodney Brooks, who joined MIT in the 1980s, challenged the dominant symbolic paradigm with his behavior-based robotics approach, arguing that intelligence emerges not from internal representations but from embodied interaction with the environment. His work on subsumption architecture and robots like Genghis and Cog redefined robotics AI, shifting focus from centralized reasoning to decentralized, sensorimotor loops\u2014a philosophy that underpins modern autonomous systems from drones to self-driving cars.\n\nMIT\u2019s influence accelerated with the rise of machine learning in the 1990s and 2000s, particularly through its pioneering work in probabilistic modeling and statistical learning. Researchers like Tommi Jaakkola, Leslie Kaelbling, and Andrew Ng (who began his Ph.D. at MIT before moving to Stanford) helped bridge the gap between symbolic AI and data-driven methods. Jaakkola\u2019s work on variational inference and graphical models provided tools for handling uncertainty in complex systems\u2014critical for real-world AI applications. Kaelbling\u2019s contributions to reinforcement learning, especially her work on partially observable Markov decision processes (POMDPs), laid the theoretical groundwork for modern decision-making agents in robotics and game AI. Ng\u2019s early work on neural networks and machine learning algorithms, including his advocacy for scaling up deep learning with GPUs, foreshadowed the deep learning revolution that would erupt a decade later.\