grawal on curiosity-driven exploration in reinforcement learning, and by Jacob Andreas on language grounding in embodied agents points toward the next frontier: AI that is not just powerful, but reliable, interpretable, and adaptable. The quest for artificial general intelligence (AGI), once considered speculative, is now being approached with renewed rigor at MIT\u2014through integrative architectures that combine neural networks with symbolic reasoning, causal inference, and developmental learning models inspired by child cognition.\n\nIn essence, MIT\u2019s influence on AI research and discoveries is not a series of isolated achievements but a continuous, self-reinforcing cycle: bold ideas attract brilliant minds, who build tools and systems that enable further discovery, which in turn draws more talent, funding, and collaboration. It is an institution where the question is not merely \u201cCan we build it?\u201d but \u201cShould we build it\u2014and how will it change what it means to be human?\u201d This dual commitment to technical excellence and ethical reflection is what makes MIT not just a leader in AI, but its conscience and its compass. As AI continues to reshape economies, societies, and even the nature of intelligence itself, MIT\u2019s role\u2014as originator, innovator, critic, and guide\u2014will remain indispensable. Its legacy is not in the algorithms it invented, but in the mindset it cultivated: one that dares to imagine machines that think, learns from their failures, and insists that progress must serve humanity."
